    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. Can you expand on the 'active listening' part? Specifically, how should a nurse handle it when a patient is being really irrational or acting in a way that just feels... unpredictable and erratic? Like, they aren't making sense and their behavior is just making everyone in the room feel tense and on edge.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When dealing with irrational or erratic behavior, active listening shifts from understanding the *logic* of the words to acknowledging the *emotion* behind them. You can't argue with the chaos, so instead, you mirror the feeling, like saying, \"I can see you are incredibly frustrated right now,\" even if the reason they are frustrated is nonsensical. This is about validating the *feeling*—the anger, the fear—not the *fact* of what they are saying. It's a way of saying, \"I hear you, even if I don't understand the whole story.\"",
      "source": "target",
